1
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:19,300
A hundred and fifty million years ago, the world was warmer, and greener, and it belonged to giants.

2
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:30,550
This is Brachiosaurus. She stands taller than a four storey building, and she spends almost every waking hour eating.

3
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:38,700
Her long neck lets her reach the tops of the conifers, where no other animal can compete.

4
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:47,210
Every day, she strips hundreds of kilograms of needles from these trees, and she never really stops growing.

5
00:00:54,610 --> 00:00:59,890
Below her, a Stegosaurus and her young are grazing on ferns.

6
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:09,460
The great plates along her back may help her to warm up in the morning sun. But it is the tail that matters.

7
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:22,430
Four long spikes, swung with enough force to pierce bone. Paleontologists call it the thagomizer, a name borrowed, rather fittingly, from a cartoon.

8
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:37,550
Seventy five million years later, a herd of Parasaurolophus has come down to the river to drink.

9
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,760
That long, hollow crest is not a weapon. It is a trumpet.

10
00:01:44,010 --> 00:01:50,850
Each call is a little different, and the herd use them to keep in touch across the reeds and the mist.

11
00:01:52,100 --> 00:02:02,460
Scientists who have built models of the crest say it would have made a deep, mournful sound, like a horn across the water.

12
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:13,380
Over the coast, the skies belong to Teranodon.

13
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:21,410
With wings six metres across, it can glide for hours, barely beating them at all.

14
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:31,900
And despite its looks, it is not a dinosaur at all, but a distant cousin. A flying reptile that has mastered the air.

15
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:40,230
It feeds at sea, snatching fish from close to the surface, and returning to the cliffs to rest.

16
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:52,910
In the deserts of what is now Mongolia, a different kind of hunter is at work.

17
00:02:54,160 --> 00:03:02,240
Velociraptor. Forget what you may have seen in the films. It is the size of a large turkey, and it is covered in feathers.

18
00:03:03,490 --> 00:03:09,310
But it is fast, and it is clever, and a lone Protoceratops makes a tempting target.

19
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:21,240
This time, the Protoceratops stands its ground. And the raptor, deciding the meal is not worth the risk, slips away into the dunes.

20
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:35,620
Sixty six million years ago, on the plains of North America, a herd of Triceratops is feeding.

21
00:03:36,870 --> 00:03:39,430
They are not alone.

22
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:49,840
Tyrannosaurus rex. Twelve metres long, with the strongest bite of any land animal that has ever lived.

23
00:03:51,090 --> 00:04:04,610
But a Triceratops is no easy prey. Three horns, and a shield of solid bone. The tyrannosaur watches, and waits, and for today at least, decides to look elsewhere.

24
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:12,920
What none of them can know is that the sky above them is about to change for ever.

25
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:23,240
And yet, the dinosaurs never quite left.

26
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:33,650
Their descendants are all around us, singing in our gardens every morning. Every bird you see is a living dinosaur.

27
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:41,140
The little sparrow on the bird bath shares a family tree with Tyrannosaurus rex.

28
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:46,850
And that, perhaps, is the most remarkable story of all.

